    [*Cues*], [*Notes*],
  ),
  [What was the Old Regime?],
  [
    - Society divided into three estates
    - First Estate: clergy, about 1% of the population, paid no main tax
    - Second Estate: nobility, held most high offices and land, also largely exempt
    - Third Estate: everyone else, about 97%, paid nearly all taxes
  ],

  [Why was the government in debt?],
  [
    - Costly wars, including Seven Years' War and support for the American Revolution
    - Royal spending at Versailles was very high
    - Tax system was unfair and inefficient, so revenue could not keep up
    - Interest on debt took a large share of the budget
  ],

  [Who paid the taxes, and why was that a problem?],
  [
    - Third Estate peasants and urban workers carried the tax burden
    - Privileged estates resisted reform, so proposals to tax them equally failed
    - Resentment grew over unfair privileges
  ],

  [How did Enlightenment ideas contribute?],
  [
    - Thinkers such as Montesquieu, Voltaire and Rousseau criticised absolute monarchy and privilege
    - Ideas of natural rights, equality before the law and popular sovereignty spread through books and salons
    - Gave the Third Estate a vocabulary for demanding change
  ],

  [What role did bad harvests and bread prices play?],
  [
    - Poor harvests in 1788 and 1789 raised bread prices sharply
    - Bread was the main food of poorer families, so hunger spread quickly
    - Food shortages fuelled unrest in towns and countryside
  ],

  [What happened in 1789?],
  [
    - Louis XVI summoned the Estates-General, the first since 1614
    - Third Estate demanded voting by head instead of by estate, and declared itself the National Assembly
    - Tennis Court Oath: deputies vowed not to disband until France had a constitution
    - Storming of the Bastille on 14 July 1789 became a symbol of revolt
  ],
)

= Summary

#answer-space(height: 6em)

#callout(kind: "remember")[
  Long-term causes (social structure, unfair taxes, Enlightenment ideas) built pressure over decades. Short-term causes (debt crisis, bad harvests, the Estates-General) turned that pressure into revolution in 1789.
]